# 001: ARCHITECTURE & CONTEXT PROTOCOL

## 1. Use the appropriate current authority

- For **Genezisi studio homepage / Infinite Website** work, first read `AGENTS.md` and `docs/infinite-website/NORTH_STAR_v1.2.md` with its visitor flow and acceptance gates. The old V2 “Signal” specs are historical, not an equal choice.
- For the separate **digital-card** product, consult its own specs and `docs/MEMORY.md` index. Preserve its existing flows.
- Start with a scoped reading of the relevant code, but when asked for an **audit of all documentation or cross-cutting rules**, inspect the complete relevant documentation inventory instead of refusing under a token-economy shortcut.
- Avoid rewriting unaffected logic. State the files to be touched, preserved boundaries, implementation rationale, actual tests and unresolved risks.

## 2. Actual framework and state

- The recorded baseline is **Next.js 16.2.1 / React 19.2.3**, not Next.js 15. Inspect `package.json` and app structure before working.
- Prefer React Server Components for server-rendered work and client isolation for DOM animation/WebGL or client interaction. Use established app patterns; don't introduce Partial Prerendering or Server Actions as unconditional requirements.
- Do not use React effects for redundant data loading when server data or existing patterns suffice. Avoid SSR/client hydration mismatches from motion and dynamic initial states.
- Maintain deterministic state shapes for any existing Server Actions; never replace API routes just to satisfy a generic architecture recipe.

## 3. Preserve quality and release safety

- Use recoverable branches and minimize unrelated churn. Preserve actual local unpublished work and authentic site content.
- Do not confuse an opt-in HTML proof with real Next.js route integration or production acceptance. Capture browser/device evidence of the animation and fallback paths.
- No `main`, `preview-*`, or deployment changes without explicit founder authorization.
